<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Build the Basic HTML Document Structure</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">A complete HTML page follows a basic document structure. The first line, &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;, tells the browser to use modern HTML. The &lt;html&gt; element wraps the whole document. Inside it, the &lt;head&gt; stores information about the page, while the &lt;body&gt; contains the content visitors normally see. Most of your visible webpage work will happen inside the body.</p><p>The &lt;title&gt; inside the head names the browser tab. The character-set meta element helps the browser interpret text correctly, and the viewport meta element helps the page scale on phones and tablets. These lines are not visually exciting, but they are part of a dependable starting template. Notice also that most HTML elements have an opening tag and a closing tag, and nested elements are indented so their relationships are easier to read.</p><p>HTML describes meaning before appearance. At this stage, the page may look plain because CSS has not been added. That is okay. Your first goal is a correctly organized document that the browser can understand. Styling comes after the structure is in place.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Put page information in &lt;head&gt;.</li><li>Put visible page content in &lt;body&gt;.</li><li>Nest tags carefully and keep indentation consistent.</li><li>Do not judge the HTML by appearance yet; structure comes first.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">In the editor, change the browser title, replace the visible heading, and add a second paragraph inside the body. Keep every visible element between &lt;body&gt; and &lt;/body&gt;.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe class="figma-code-lab" loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-04-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: HTML"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: Where should a visible paragraph normally be placed?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">Inside the &lt;body&gt; element.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 4 of 20</div>
</div>","textFeedbackInput":"Show Feedback","textFeedbackTextarea":"","textInfoDurationInput":"","textInfoDurationTextInput":"Duration","textInfoParticipantsInput":"","textInfoParticipantsTextInput":"Grouping"}

