<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Add Headings, Paragraphs, Links, and Images</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">Your project becomes useful when HTML gives the content meaningful structure. Headings organize topics, paragraphs hold ordinary text, links connect visitors to another location, and images communicate visual information. Use &lt;h1&gt; for the primary page heading and lower heading levels for sections beneath it. Use &lt;p&gt; for paragraphs rather than inserting repeated line breaks simply to create space.</p><p>A link uses the &lt;a&gt; element and an href destination. Link text should explain where the visitor will go; “MDN Web Development” is more informative than “click here.” An image uses &lt;img&gt; with a src location and an alt text alternative. Meaningful images need concise alternative text so the information is available when the image cannot be seen. Decorative images can use an empty alt value.</p><p>The example now looks much closer to a real webpage. It has a header, navigation links, two main sections, an image, a list of learning resources, and a footer. The appearance is still intentionally simple. In the next section, CSS will style this same structure instead of replacing it.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Choose headings based on content hierarchy.</li><li>Write descriptive link text.</li><li>Give meaningful images useful alt text.</li><li>Use semantic sections to keep the page understandable.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Change the main heading, add another paragraph in the About section, and add one new list item with a link. Then improve the image's alt text without describing unnecessary visual details.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe class="figma-code-lab" loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-05-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: HTML"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: What is the purpose of an image's alt attribute?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">It provides a text alternative for the meaningful information communicated by the image.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 5 of 20</div>
