<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Understand CSS and Connect Styles to HTML</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">CSS changes how existing HTML is presented. A CSS rule has a selector and one or more declarations. In h1 { color: teal; }, h1 is the selector, color is the property, and teal is the value. The selector connects the rule to matching HTML. This relationship is why clear HTML classes and elements become useful when a page grows.</p><p>In a traditional project, CSS is often stored in a separate file such as styles.css and connected from the HTML head with &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"&gt;. The embedded editor gives CSS its own pane and automatically applies that pane to the preview. Even though you do not manually create styles.css here, think of the CSS pane as that stylesheet. The HTML remains responsible for structure while CSS controls presentation.</p><p>The starter rules below change the body font, header colors, and link color without modifying the HTML content. Experiment with one value and notice that the words and elements stay the same. This separation is important: you can redesign a page without rewriting its information.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>A selector decides which HTML receives a style.</li><li>Properties and values describe the visual change.</li><li>External CSS is commonly linked from the HTML head.</li><li>Structure and presentation should remain conceptually separate.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Change the header background, link color, and font family. Do not edit the HTML pane. Observe how much the page can change through CSS alone.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe class="figma-code-lab" loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-07-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: CSS Connection"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: What connects a CSS rule to an HTML element?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">The CSS selector identifies which matching HTML element or elements receive the rule.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 7 of 20</div>
</div>","textFeedbackInput":"Show Feedback","textFeedbackTextarea":"","textInfoDurationInput":"","textInfoDurationTextInput":"Duration","textInfoParticipantsInput":"","textInfoParticipantsTextInput":"Grouping"}

