<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Add Colors and Fonts</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">Color and typography create visual identity, but their first job is readability. CSS can set text color, background color, font family, font size, weight, and line height. A small, consistent palette usually produces a stronger design than many unrelated colors. In this project, a dark neutral, teal accent, white surface, and light background create clear visual roles.</p><p>Contrast matters. Text must be easy to distinguish from its background, and links should remain recognizable. Avoid using color as the only signal for an important state. The example navigation links are white and bold against a dark header, while ordinary links use a teal color against white. Font size and line height also help learners scan longer explanations without crowding the page.</p><p>CSS lets you change these choices without altering the document's meaning. The &lt;h1&gt; remains the primary heading whether it is 32 or 52 pixels tall. This is one reason you should choose HTML elements for structure and CSS for appearance. As you edit, compare the result with the original and ask whether your new combination is easier or harder to read.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Use a small, purposeful color palette.</li><li>Maintain strong text/background contrast.</li><li>Use font size and line height to support readability.</li><li>Do not choose an HTML element merely for its default appearance.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Change the main header color, heading size, and body line height. Keep the page readable, then compare your version with Reset.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe class="figma-code-lab" loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-08-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: CSS"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: Should you use an &lt;h3&gt; just because you like its default size?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">No. Choose heading levels for structure, then control the visual size with CSS.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 8 of 20</div>
</div>","textFeedbackInput":"Show Feedback","textFeedbackTextarea":"","textInfoDurationInput":"","textInfoDurationTextInput":"Duration","textInfoParticipantsInput":"","textInfoParticipantsTextInput":"Grouping"}

