<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">JavaScript Practice and Knowledge Check</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">This checkpoint combines the JavaScript ideas used in the course project. The HTML already creates the button and message. The CSS already makes the page readable. Your task is to work only in the JavaScript pane and make the existing button produce a useful response. Start by selecting the button and paragraph, then attach a click listener to the button, and finally update the paragraph's textContent inside the callback.</p><p>After the basic interaction works, make one small improvement: change the button label after the tip appears. This demonstrates that JavaScript can update more than one element during the same event. Keep the behavior easy to describe. A user should understand what the button does from its label and feedback.</p><p>Complete the knowledge check after testing the interaction several times. If your code does not run, read the error shown in the output preview and compare names character by character. The most important outcome is not memorizing the exact syntax; it is understanding the sequence of selecting elements, listening for an event, and updating the DOM.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
