<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Completed Webpage, Resources, and Next Steps</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">You have completed the guided path from a plain HTML document to a styled, interactive webpage. The final version below contains the same features introduced throughout the module: meaningful page structure, headings and paragraphs, navigation and resource links, an image with alternative text, responsive CSS, a clear visual hierarchy, and a JavaScript button response. The important achievement is understanding how those pieces connect, not simply reaching a particular visual design.</p><p>Continue by rebuilding the project without copying the starter code. Then make one controlled extension. You might add another section, create a second button with a different response, add a simple form field, or redesign the page while keeping the HTML semantics intact. When you encounter unfamiliar syntax, use a reference rather than trying to memorize the entire language. MDN Web Docs, W3Schools, freeCodeCamp, and Eloquent JavaScript are useful starting points for further practice.</p><p>To publish this course project, export the eXeLearning project as a website, test the navigation and interactive editors in more than one browser, and upload the exported site to the hosting location your team selected. Before submission, proofread the pages, verify external links, test the final button and assessments, and confirm that the public URL remains accessible through the required date.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Rebuild the page from memory as your first follow-up challenge.</li><li>Add one new feature at a time.</li><li>Use reputable references when you need unfamiliar syntax.</li><li>Test the exported course and public link before submission.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Interact with the completed page one final time. Then choose one next-step feature you could add and identify whether it belongs in HTML, CSS, or JavaScript.</p>
</div>
</div>
